id: mental-model:dave:number-sense
title: "Dave / number sense"
kind: mental-model
status: draft-0.1
spec_version: mental-model.v0.1
template_ref: templates/mental-model.yawn
principal_ref: principal:dave
arena: "Kindergarten math at home with Dave's daughter"
updated: 2026-09-09

questions:
  - key: role
    answer: "A dad who shows one thing, waits, then asks. Not a teacher who tells."
  - key: relevance
    answer: >
      She meets how-many every day: snacks, stairs, fingers. If I can see what
      she sees, I can meet her where she is instead of where the worksheet is.
  - key: definition
    answer: "Feeling how many before saying the counting words."
  - key: inside
    answer: "Eight small skills that hide inside one word."
  - key: show
    answer: "Six ordinary objects, each with one question."
  - key: assumptions
    answer: "What I am taking for granted, marked so I can correct it."
  - key: proof
    answer: "What she does, not what I explain."
  - key: lacuna
    answer: "Where she actually is on each piece is unknown until I look."

concept:
  name: Number sense
  one_line: "Knowing how many, and how much more, without needing to count every time."
  inside:
    - name: Subitize
      glyph: "• •  •"
      one_line: "See one to four at a glance."
    - name: One-to-one
      glyph: "| | |"
      one_line: "One touch, one counting word."
    - name: Stable order
      glyph: "1 2 3"
      one_line: "The words always come in the same order."
    - name: Cardinality
      glyph: "= 4"
      one_line: "The last word says how many."
    - name: Compare
      glyph: "••• < •••••"
      one_line: "More, fewer, same."
    - name: Part and whole
      glyph: "2 + 3"
      one_line: "Five hides inside as two and three."
    - name: Invariance
      glyph: "• • • • •"
      one_line: "Spread out, still five."
    - name: Order
      glyph: "3 → 4"
      one_line: "Comes before, comes after."

show:
  - icon: Dice
    do: "Roll one die. Cover it fast."
    say: "How many? Don't count, just look."
  - icon: Fingers
    do: "Hold up a hand."
    say: "Show me four. Now show me four a different way."
  - icon: Two piles
    do: "Make piles of three and five crackers."
    say: "Which pile has more? How do you know?"
  - icon: Five frame
    do: "Draw five boxes, fill three."
    say: "How many are missing?"
  - icon: Spread row
    do: "Count five blocks, then spread them apart."
    say: "Still five? Or did it change?"
  - icon: Sharing
    do: "Give two to her, two to me."
    say: "How many altogether? What if I give you one of mine?"

embody:
  - "Ask first, tell last."
  - "She says the number. I say what I noticed."
  - "Show one thing. Wait. Then the question."
  - "A wrong answer is an observation, not a failure: 'You said six. Let's look together.'"

assumptions:
  - text: "She is five or six and already knows the counting words to ten."
    epistemic_status: assumed
  - text: "She learns by handling things, not by hearing about them."
    epistemic_status: assumed
  - text: "Ten minutes of play beats thirty minutes of drill."
    epistemic_status: assumed
  - text: "Number sense is partly innate approximate magnitude and partly built through language and practice."
    epistemic_status: inferred
    epistemic_note: "From the sources below, not from observing her."

proof:
  signals:
    - "She says 'four' for a dice face without counting."
    - "She counts a row, hears 'how many?', and answers without recounting."
    - "She picks the bigger pile at a glance and can say why."
    - "She says 'still five' after I spread them out."
  falsifier: "If she counts every time, she is not subitizing yet. Go smaller, go slower."

lacunae:
  - "Where is she right now on each of the eight pieces?"
  - "Which objects does she reach for on her own?"
  - "Does it carry from snacks to blocks to stairs?"

sources:
  - text: "Gelman and Gallistel, The Child's Understanding of Number (1978): counting principles."
    epistemic_status: reported
  - text: "Clements and Sarama, Learning and Teaching Early Math: learning trajectories."
    epistemic_status: reported
  - text: "Dehaene, The Number Sense (1997): approximate number system."
    epistemic_status: reported
